# Overview
#
# Each phrase points at a passage by where it sits (`described above`,
# `the next section`), so the reference breaks when someone moves the
# passage or adds one between. The reader then follows it to the wrong
# passage. The fix links the section by name. The rule sits at
# Recommended, because a phrase can also point at the reader's own note
# or follow a section linked by name.
#
# Expression
#
# `verbs` lists the verbs that count before `above` and `below`
# (`described above`, `see below`). `since` lists the verbs that count
# before `earlier`, `previously`, and `later` (`mentioned earlier`).
# Before a word that points back (`above`, `earlier`, `previously`),
# each list counts only when `as` does not come first. Weir has no
# lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before the verb and
# narrows the finding back to the phrase.
#
# `the section above`, `the next section`, `the previous paragraph`, and
# their kin count as written. `passage` lists the nouns that name a
# passage, and `the above` counts before one of them (`the above
# scenario`, `the preceding risks`). It also counts as the object of a
# word that takes the whole passage (`given the above`, `in addition to
# the above`).
#
# `docs` lists the nouns for a document or a part of one. `earlier in
# this guide` and `later in this chapter` count with each of them,
# except before a comma and the subject of a clause (`Later in this
# section, you set the flag`). A clause is a subject and its verb, such
# as `the tests pass`. By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so
# `let scope "Sentence"` lets the guard cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# The rule counts references to a passage, not to a block beside the
# sentence. `the example below` and `the table above` usually sit next
# to their block, the way `the following` does, so they stay out. A bare
# `the above` stays out too, because it usually points at the code block
# right above it (`The above produces the same output`).
#
# `the paragraph above` and its kin stay out before `it` and `them`,
# because the pronoun names a nearby line or block, not the reader's
# place in the document. A comment can then describe the layout of an
# input (`a line that opens with a colon makes the paragraph above it a
# term`).
#
# `introduced` and `listed` stay out of `since`, because they name when
# a feature arrived (`Generics were introduced later`) or where an item
# sits (`variants listed later in the enum`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseBackReference reports `as described above` and `as described
# earlier`, so the filter skips those verbs after `as`. `below` and
# `later` count after `as` too (`as described below`), because
# ProseBackReference reports only the words that point back.
#
# ProseSignpost reports the opener in `Later in this section, you set
# the flag`, so the guard on `earlier in this guide` and `later in this
# chapter` skips that sentence.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 115 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 17 of 20 sampled
# hits pointed at a passage by position. The 3 misfires were of two
# kinds. One was `the ID that you noted earlier`, where the reader took
# the note. The other was `later in this chapter` after a section that
# the sentence already links by name.
expr where [above, below]
expr verbs [see, described, mentioned, explained, discussed, outlined]
expr since [described, mentioned, explained, discussed, outlined, covered, noted]
expr passage [situation, situations, scenario, scenarios, problem, problems, (use cases), tradeoffs, limitations, risks, considerations]
expr docs [guide, page, document, section, chapter, tutorial, doc, post, article, book]
expr main [
    <(!as @verbs above), (@verbs above)>,
    (@verbs below),
    (the [section, sections, paragraph, chapter] @where!(( )[it, them])),
    (the [next, previous, preceding] [section, chapter]),
    (the [above, preceding] @passage),
    ([given, following, notwithstanding, despite, besides, (in addition to), (based on)] the above),
    <(!as @since [earlier, previously]), (@since [earlier, previously])>,
    (@since later),
    (the [next, previous, preceding, prior] [paragraph, paragraphs]),
    <([earlier, later] in this @docs!(,( )[DET, PRON, PROPN, NOUN])), ([earlier, later] in this @docs)>
]
let message "Positional reference. Link the section by name."
let description "Flags `described above`, `the next section`, and similar references that point at a passage by position."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The backoff follows the restart policy in Pod Lifecycle."
allows "The header is parsed as described above."
allows "In the example below, the claim requests one device."
allows "The above produces the same output."
allows "A line that opens with a colon makes the paragraph above it a term."
allows "The headings name the sections below them."
allows "Variants listed later in the enum compare greater."
allows "Generics were introduced later, in Go 1.18."
